  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Record warmth possible on Thursday.\n\nHigh pressure will continue to drift east of New England tonight \nthrough Thursday, with deep southwesterly flow in its wake \ntransporting unseasonable warmth into the eastern Great Lakes. Highs \nwill reach the low to mid 70s for lower elevations. With the \nexpected warmth, some records may fall or be tied. See the climate \nsection of the AFD below for the current record highs for October \n30th. \n\nAdditionally, winds will strengthen Thursday as a 35 to 45 knot low \nlevel jet moves over the eastern Great Lakes. Poor low level lapse \nrates will prevent full mixing of stronger winds from aloft, but \nchanneling down Lake Erie may still support gusts of 30-40 mph \nacross the Niagara Frontier in the afternoon. Gusts of 20 to 30 mph \nare expected elsewhere.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A brief shot of light rain Thursday night. Dry \nweather then resumes and lasts through the weekend. \n\nA weak cold front nears the region Thursday, then crosses the \neastern Great Lakes Thursday night. This system is moisture starved, \nbut may have just enough forcing and moisture convergence to squeeze \nout a brief period of light showers. In terms of precipitation, \namounts look very meager with only a few hundredths of an inch at \nbest possible across Western and Central NY. Showers will exit \nbefore daybreak Friday, with high pressure then building back into \nthe region later Friday through the weekend with another extended \nstretch of dry weather.",
